1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,550
Here are three hundred and

2
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:03,612
ninety-eight cars: how heavy each

3
00:00:03,612 --> 00:00:04,900
one is, and how far it

4
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,625
goes on a gallon of fuel.

5
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:09,433
Linear regression fits one straight

6
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:10,908
line through this cloud.

7
00:00:10,908 --> 00:00:12,146
You have seen this equation a

8
00:00:12,146 --> 00:00:13,558
thousand times.

9
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:15,642
But what is the line actually

10
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:17,629
claiming? Not that every car sits

11
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:18,167
on it.

12
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:19,742
They clearly do not.

13
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:21,288
Pick a weight.

14
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:23,312
Say fifteen hundred kilograms.

15
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:24,475
Here are the cars near that

16
00:00:24,475 --> 00:00:25,300
weight.

17
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,250
Average their fuel economy, and you

18
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:29,487
get one number.

19
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:30,900
That number is what the line

20
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,225
is trying to be.

21
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:34,508
Slide the weight, and the averages

22
00:00:34,508 --> 00:00:35,721
trace a path.

23
00:00:35,721 --> 00:00:37,358
The line is the straight-line guess

24
00:00:37,358 --> 00:00:38,758
for that path: the mean of

25
00:00:38,758 --> 00:00:40,308
y, given x.

26
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:43,292
Close. Not exact.

27
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:44,392
Remember that.

28
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,462
Now look at the cars near

29
00:00:46,462 --> 00:00:47,962
fifteen hundred again.

30
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:49,525
Each one misses the line by

31
00:00:49,525 --> 00:00:50,538
some amount.

32
00:00:50,538 --> 00:00:52,900
Regression calls these residuals.

33
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,200
Their spread has a name too:

34
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,337
sigma. The textbook writes it as

35
00:00:57,337 --> 00:00:59,100
noise added to the line.

36
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,575
But that word, noise, hides the

37
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:03,313
point. The spread is not an

38
00:01:03,313 --> 00:01:04,700
error. It is part of the

39
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:05,800
prediction.

40
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:07,850
Put the two together.

41
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:09,337
At each weight, a centre from

42
00:01:09,337 --> 00:01:10,562
the line and a spread from

43
00:01:10,562 --> 00:01:12,325
sigma: that is a whole bell

44
00:01:12,325 --> 00:01:14,075
curve, one per weight.

45
00:01:14,583 --> 00:01:16,908
Ordinary least squares assumes every

46
00:01:16,908 --> 00:01:18,771
bell has the same width.

47
00:01:18,771 --> 00:01:20,633
Slide along, and watch the real

48
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:23,083
cars disagree: the spread shrinks as

49
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:24,358
they get heavier.

50
00:01:27,283 --> 00:01:29,546
So the honest statement is this.

51
00:01:29,546 --> 00:01:32,108
Given x, y has a distribution.

52
00:01:32,108 --> 00:01:33,671
Its centre moves with x, and

53
00:01:33,671 --> 00:01:35,096
so does its spread.

54
00:01:35,096 --> 00:01:36,458
The line was only ever the

55
00:01:36,458 --> 00:01:37,408
centre.

56
00:01:37,917 --> 00:01:39,742
Regression is not predicting a

57
00:01:39,742 --> 00:01:41,517
number. It is predicting a

58
00:01:41,517 --> 00:01:43,479
conditional distribution.

59
00:01:43,479 --> 00:01:45,154
Ordinary least squares is the

60
00:01:45,154 --> 00:01:47,067
special case where that distribution

61
00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:49,642
is Normal, its width is constant,

62
00:01:49,642 --> 00:01:51,742
and you only model the centre.

63
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:53,675
Everything that follows in this

64
00:01:53,675 --> 00:01:55,500
series is about dropping those three

65
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,950
assumptions, one at a time.
